[h1]ZeD-GUI [B42] | tester build 0.2.0-t10[/h1]
[b]This is a test build.[/b] It is unlisted. Report problems in the pinned [b]Bug reports[/b] discussion, one post per problem.

ZeD-GUI, the Zero Day Graphic User Interface, replaces the inventory, the character and loot windows, the HUD and the menus for Build 42. In the fiction it runs on ZNIX, a made-up platform: a custom kernel built by a paranoid do-it-yourself doomsday prepper. The look is inspired by the popular operating systems of the period, Macintosh System 6 and System 7 most of all; it is not an endorsement of Apple, Microsoft or any other brand. It works with a controller and with a mouse and keyboard.

[h2]Before you subscribe[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Build 42 only.[/b] Tested on 42.21 and the 42.22 pre-release. It does not run on Build 41.
[*][b]Use a new game or a copy of a save.[/b]
[*][b]Tested in single player only.[/b] If you try it on a server, report what happened.
[*][b]It replaces the game's inventory and character windows.[/b] Buttons that other mods add to those windows do not show while ZeD-GUI is on. Mods that use the game's own systems (context menu options, container buttons, items, recipes) work.
[*][b]It adds one item:[/b] a steel digital watch in the loot. If you remove ZeD-GUI from a save, those watches go too. Everything else is interface and can be added or removed at any time.
[*][b]It comes with a helper mod, ZeD picks,[/b] which stays empty until you pick files in Mods.
[*][b]Text is in English.[/b] Where the game has a word, ZeD-GUI uses the game's translation.
[/list]

[h2]Windows[/h2]
[b]Character window.[/b] Tabs for loot, equipment, health, temperature, ID, journal, skills and the radio you carry. The game's Health, Skills, Info and Protection keys open these tabs.

[b]World window.[/b] Tabs for loot, crafting, building, furniture, maps, foraging, animals, radios nearby and the vehicle you are next to.

Open containers show as columns side by side.

[b]Cards.[/b] Click an item, a generator, a campfire, a farm plot or a liquid for its card: what it is, what you can do, and why an action is not available.

[b]Every window[/b] has a menu box in its title bar for its look, a help line at its foot, and resizes from its edges.

[h2]HUD[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Belt:[/b] your hands side by side, the main hand bigger, with a ring for the condition of what they hold. One item in both hands: the off hand peeks from behind. A gun's rounds show on a tab under the hand. Then your gear's slots, on a strap in its material.
[*][b]Watch:[/b] a worn watch shows on the belt. Click it for the full watch: time, date, temperature, alarm and stopwatch.
[*][b]Dashboard:[/b] shows when you drive: warning lamps by condition, the key turns, ash trays open, the taxi's meter runs, and the radio opens up close.
[*][b]Moodles:[/b] placed right, left or above the belt; each explains itself in a thought bubble.
[*][b]Action bar:[/b] shows the current action and the queue in words, or as ten segments.
[*][b]Speech and thoughts:[/b] what your character says, and the action under way, show in bubbles over the head.
[*][b]Messages:[/b] notes from other mods go into one window instead of covering the game; errors are counted per mod.
[/list]

[h2]Maps[/h2]
Three styles: survey, color and a paper road map, and the game's map key. Flyers you have read are pinned on the map.

[h2]Menus[/h2]
The main menu is a desk with a monitor. Load, new game, mods, options, sandbox, character creation and server settings open as windows on the monitor.

[b]Mods.[/b] The list flags missing requirements, the wrong game version and conflicts between mods (which copy the game uses, and why). A mod's info pane has tabs: About (what turning it off does to a save), Options (changed in place, with Reset settings), Sandbox (what it adds to a new game) and Conflicts. When two mods have the same code file, you can pick whose copy runs. Profiles save a mod list with its load order.

[h2]Controller[/h2]
[list]
[*]The D-pad moves between controls. A is left click, X is right click (the context menu), B backs out. Y opens and hides both windows; LB and RB pick one.
[*]With no window open, hold a D-pad direction for a wheel: Left is Character, Right is World, Up is Social, Down is Here and now. Point with the right stick and let go of the D-pad to take. A slice with more opens in place: A takes, B goes back.
[*]Hold Select for the ZeD wheel: theme, accent color, text size, UI scale, moodles, action bar, Messages, reset windows, ZeD setup and safe mode.
[*]Quick actions: in the world, hold LB or RB and press A, B, X, Y, L3 or R3 for one of twelve actions, set in ZeD setup or from a wheel.
[*]The buttons are drawn for your controller: Xbox, PlayStation, Steam Deck or Nintendo (ZeD setup, Gamepad glyph display).
[*]In the driver's seat the D-pad opens the wheels. World wheel, Vehicle, Dashboard gives the dashboard the controller.
[*]Aiming with the right stick hides both windows; Y shows them again.
[*]Camera zoom: hold LB and tap RB to zoom out, hold RB and tap LB to zoom in.
[*]Map: RT and LT zoom; the left stick moves the cursor and pans at the edge.
[*]If your Steam Input layout binds the bumpers to zoom, remove those bindings.
[/list]

[h2]Options[/h2]
ZeD setup is the first page: dark, light or high contrast theme, nine accent colors, two text sizes, a UI scale, see-through windows, the controller highlight, and folding away actions you can't do yet. The game's own pages follow, and mark the settings ZeD-GUI replaces.

[b]Sandbox options in game[/b] (ZeD setup, Sandbox; off until you turn it on): change this save's sandbox settings from the pause window. Single player only.

[h2]Getting started[/h2]
[olist]
[*]Subscribe.
[*]Main menu, Mods: turn on [b]ZeD-GUI[/b]. Put it after other interface mods in the load order.
[*]Quit the game and start it again. ZeD-GUI loads its fonts when the game starts.
[*]Start a new game, or load a copy of a save.
[*]Options, ZeD setup: set the look and the HUD.
[*]The inventory key opens the Character window, the loot key the World window.
[/olist]

[h2]Reporting a problem[/h2]
Post in the pinned [b]Bug reports[/b] discussion. Include:
[list]
[*]The version: 0.2.0-t10. It is shown in Mods, and in the desk's ZeD menu under About.
[*]Your game version and your mod list.
[*]What you did, what you expected, and what happened. Add a screenshot if you can.
[*]Your console.txt: [i]C:\Users\(you)\Zomboid\console.txt[/i] on Windows, [i]~/Zomboid/console.txt[/i] on Linux and macOS. The game replaces this file each time it starts, so copy it before you restart. Upload it to a paste site and post the link.
[/list]
[b]Safe mode:[/b] if ZeD-GUI stops you from playing, turn on Options, ZeD setup, Safe mode. The game's own screens return. Mention it in your report.
Play without the [i]-debug[/i] launch option unless asked: with it the game stops on every Lua error, other mods' included.

[h2]Server admins[/h2]
Mod ID: ZeD_UI
Every player needs it, because it adds an item.

[h2]Credits[/h2]
Made by Hermit. ZeD-GUI has been in development since Build 41 went stable. Every system, design and piece of logic was built to Hermit's vision. AI was used as a tool: Claude made it possible for one person to build a project that used to be out of scope for a solo modder. AI is the tool. Hermit is the maker.

Fonts: Fira Sans and Fira Mono (SIL Open Font License). Icons: ZeD-GUI's own pixel art.

Project Zomboid is made by The Indie Stone. ZeD-GUI is a fan-made mod, not affiliated with The Indie Stone, Apple or Microsoft.

